Lab Material
Lab: Implementing CI/CD for Multiple Environments
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

â¢ Structure a project repository to support multiple deployment environments (development, staging, production) â¢ Create environment-specific configuration files using Kubernetes/OpenShift ConfigMaps â¢ Write parameterized shell scripts that build, deploy, and test an application per environment â¢ Configure a GitHub Actions workflow that promotes a build through dev, staging, and production stages â¢ Troubleshoot common configuration and deployment issues in a multi-environment pipeline

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

â¢ Basic understanding of Git version control â¢ Familiarity with containerization concepts (Docker) â¢ Knowledge of YAML syntax and configuration files â¢ A basic understanding of Kubernetes/OpenShift objects (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap) â¢ Access to a Linux-based environment with the following tools installed:

git --version
docker --version
oc version --client
If any of these are missing on a Debian/Ubuntu-based system, install them with:

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y git docker.io
The OpenShift CLI (oc) install method varies by distribution; if it is not already present in your lab environment, follow your organization's standard installation instructions for oc before continuing. This lab assumes oc is already configured and logged in to a cluster (oc whoami should return a username). If you do not have cluster access, you can still complete Tasks 1 and 2 fully, and treat Task 3's oc commands as read-only reference commands to review rather than execute.

Task 1: Setting Up the Multi-Environment Project Structure
Subtask 1.1: Create the Project and Sample Application
Create the project directory and initialize Git:
mkdir -p ~/multi-env-cicd
cd ~/multi-env-cicd
git init
git config user.name "Lab Student"
git config user.email "student@example.com"
Create the directory structure:
mkdir -p src config/dev config/staging config/prod
mkdir -p manifests/dev manifests/staging manifests/prod
mkdir -p scripts .github/workflows
Verify the structure:
find . -type d -not -path './.git*' | sort
Expected output:

.
./.github
./.github/workflows
./config
./config/dev
./config/prod
./config/staging
./manifests
./manifests/dev
./manifests/prod
./manifests/staging
./scripts
