Task 3: Building Parameterized Pipeline Scripts
Subtask 3.1: Build, Deploy, and Test Scripts
Create a parameterized build script:
cat > scripts/build.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -e

ENVIRONMENT=${1:-dev}
IMAGE_TAG=${2:-1.0.0}
IMAGE_NAME="multi-env-app"

echo "Building application for environment: $ENVIRONMENT"
echo "Image tag: ${ENVIRONMENT}-${IMAGE_TAG}"

cd src
docker build -t ${IMAGE_NAME}:${ENVIRONMENT}-${IMAGE_TAG} .
cd ..

echo "Build completed: ${IMAGE_NAME}:${ENVIRONMENT}-${IMAGE_TAG}"
EOF
chmod +x scripts/build.sh
Create a parameterized deploy script:
cat > scripts/deploy.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -e

ENVIRONMENT=${1:-dev}
IMAGE_TAG=${2:-1.0.0}
NAMESPACE="multi-env-${ENVIRONMENT}"

echo "Deploying to environment: $ENVIRONMENT (namespace: $NAMESPACE)"

oc create namespace "$NAMESPACE" --dry-run=client -o yaml | oc apply -f -

oc apply -f "config/${ENVIRONMENT}/app-config.yaml"

sed "s/${ENVIRONMENT}-1.0.0/${ENVIRONMENT}-${IMAGE_TAG}/g" \
  "manifests/${ENVIRONMENT}/deployment.yaml" > "/tmp/deployment-${ENVIRONMENT}.yaml"

oc apply -f "/tmp/deployment-${ENVIRONMENT}.yaml"
oc apply -f "manifests/${ENVIRONMENT}/service.yaml"

oc rollout status "deployment/multi-env-app" -n "$NAMESPACE" --timeout=180s

echo "Deployment to $ENVIRONMENT completed successfully."
EOF
chmod +x scripts/deploy.sh
Create a parameterized test script that checks the health endpoint from inside the cluster:
cat > scripts/test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -e

ENVIRONMENT=${1:-dev}
NAMESPACE="multi-env-${ENVIRONMENT}"

echo "Running tests for environment: $ENVIRONMENT"

oc wait --for=condition=available "deployment/multi-env-app" \
  -n "$NAMESPACE" --timeout=120s

echo "Checking /health endpoint..."
oc exec -n "$NAMESPACE" "deployment/multi-env-app" -- \
  wget -qO- http://localhost:3000/health

echo ""
echo "All tests passed for environment: $ENVIRONMENT"
EOF
chmod +x scripts/test.sh
Review the scripts for correctness (no cluster required for this step):
bash -n scripts/build.sh && echo "build.sh syntax OK"
bash -n scripts/deploy.sh && echo "deploy.sh syntax OK"
bash -n scripts/test.sh && echo "test.sh syntax OK"
Deliverable: Three executable scripts in scripts/ that each accept an environment name (dev, staging, or prod) as their first argument. bash -n should report no syntax errors for all three.

Subtask 3.2: Create the GitHub Actions Multi-Environment Workflow
Create the workflow file that builds once and promotes through dev, staging, and production:
cat > .github/workflows/multi-env-cicd.yml << 'EOF'
name: Multi-Environment CI/CD Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main, develop ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ main ]

env:
  IMAGE_NAME: multi-env-app

jobs:
  build-and-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '18'

      - name: Install dependencies
        working-directory: src
        run: npm install

      - name: Run unit tests
        working-directory: src
        run: npm test

      - name: Build Docker image
        run: |
          cd src
          docker build -t ${{ env.IMAGE_NAME }}:dev-${{ github.sha }} .

  deploy-dev:
    needs: build-and-test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/develop' || github.event_name == 'pull_request'
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Deploy to development
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/deploy.sh dev ${{ github.sha }}"
      - name: Test development deployment
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/test.sh dev"

  deploy-staging:
    needs: deploy-dev
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/develop'
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Deploy to staging
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/deploy.sh staging ${{ github.sha }}"
      - name: Test staging deployment
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/test.sh staging"

  deploy-production:
    needs: deploy-staging
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Deploy to production
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/deploy.sh prod ${{ github.sha }}"
      - name: Test production deployment
        run: |
          echo "Would run: ./scripts/test.sh prod"
EOF
Validate the workflow YAML is syntactically correct:
python3 -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('.github/workflows/multi-env-cicd.yml'))" && echo "Workflow YAML is valid"
Commit the completed project:
git add .
git commit -m "Add multi-environment CI/CD structure, manifests, scripts, and workflow"
Deliverable: A valid .github/workflows/multi-env-cicd.yml file with four jobs (build-and-test, deploy-dev, deploy-staging, deploy-production) chained with needs, and a Git commit containing the full project. Running the Python validation command should print Workflow YAML is valid.

Expected Outcomes
â¢ A complete local repository with environment-scoped ConfigMaps, Deployments, and Services for dev, staging, and production â¢ Three working parameterized shell scripts (build.sh, deploy.sh, test.sh) that behave differently per environment argument without code duplication â¢ A syntactically valid GitHub Actions workflow that demonstrates promotion order: build/test, then dev, then staging, then production, gated by branch conditions

Troubleshooting
Issue 1: oc apply fails with "namespace not found" or similar authorization error. Symptom: deploy.sh errors out on the oc create namespace or oc apply step. Diagnostic hint: run oc whoami and oc get namespaces to confirm you are logged in and have permission to create namespaces; if you have no cluster access, treat Task 3's deploy/test steps as a dry run and verify script logic with bash -n instead.

Issue 2: The sed substitution in deploy.sh does not update the image tag. Symptom: oc get deployment ... -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}' still shows the default tag (e.g., dev-1.0.0) after deploying with a different IMAGE_TAG. Diagnostic hint: confirm the pattern in sed exactly matches the string present in the corresponding manifests/<env>/deployment.yaml image line; the placeholder tag in each manifest must match what the script searches for.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a self-contained multi-environment CI/CD project from the ground up: a directory structure separating configuration and manifests for dev, staging, and production; a small Node.js application with a health endpoint; environment-specific ConfigMaps, Deployments, and Services with appropriately scaled resources and replica counts; and a set of parameterized shell scripts that build, deploy, and test the application against any target environment without duplicating logic. You also authored and validated a GitHub Actions workflow that enforces a realistic promotion order, only advancing a build to staging and production after it succeeds in earlier stages. Through this process you practiced the core discipline behind safe multi-environment delivery: keeping environment differences isolated in configuration rather than in code, and gating promotion on automated verification at each stage. As a next step, you could extend this pipeline with real secrets management (for example, OpenShift Secrets or a vault integration), automated rollback triggers based on failed health checks, and manual approval gates before production deployment.
